        ],
        "when": "Illinois exemptions are allowable and the asset is personal property owned by the debtor.",
        "procedure": "Retain 735 Ill. Comp. Stat. 5/12-1001(b) independently for every personal-property asset, including a vehicle or an actual trade tool. Add subsection (a) for necessary wearing apparel, bibles, school books, or family pictures; subsection (c) for a motor vehicle; subsection (d) for actual tools or books of the debtor's trade; and subsection (e) for professionally prescribed health aids. Stated business use can establish subsection (d) and does not remove subsection (b).",
        "avoid_when": "Do not apply these personal-property provisions to real property. Do not call jewelry wearing apparel or ordinary household property a trade tool, and do not omit subsection (b) merely because a specific subsection applies."
      },
      {
        "tasks": [
          "EV"
        ],
        "when": "Illinois exemptions are allowable and the asset is personal property owned by the debtor.",
        "procedure": "Evaluate 735 Ill. Comp. Stat. 5/12-1001(b) independently, including for vehicles and actual trade tools, and then retain every supported specific subsection. For the whole named asset, subsection (b) protects up to $4,000 for one debtor or $8,000 for two joint debtors; subsection (c) protects up to $2,400 for a vehicle; and subsection (d) protects up to $1,500 for one debtor or $3,000 for two joint debtors. Under each alternative, claim no more than the whole asset value.",
        "avoid_when": "Do not exclude a trade tool from subsection (b) because business use is stated, omit subsection (d) when the facts establish a trade tool, double subsection (c), or use these personal-property provisions for real property."
      }
    ],
